WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.331 --> 00:00:03.581
(upbeat ambient music)

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.110
- Hi, I'm Jacob Andreas, I'm
an assistant professor at MIT

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.750
and ECS and CSAIL and I work on

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.220
natural language processing.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.860
So sort of broadly, my
group is interested both in

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.470
developing better technologies,
better machine learning

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.530
models for working with
data from human languages

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.360
and looking at what we
can do with language

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.760
in support of other kinds
of artificial intelligence

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.593
applications.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.160
Doing things like building
robots that you can

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.950
teach using natural language
or computer vision systems

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.530
that can explain their
decisions also in language

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.270
and today I'm gonna be
answering your questions

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.040
about large language models,
what are large language models

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.780
and how do they generate text?

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.120
When you're typing on your
phone and you see that

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.190
little sort of line that
pops up with some guesses

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.320
about the word that's
gonna come next, that's a

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.301
language model and sort
of fundamentally what most

00:00:52.301 --> 00:00:53.400
of the language models that we work with

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.950
are just predictive models
of text models that have

00:00:56.829 --> 00:00:58.230
been trained on enormous
amounts of text data

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.060
to look at a sentence or
the beginning of a sentence

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.220
or look at the document in the beginning

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.950
of a document and just
predict what words are likely

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.780
to come next, how are LLMs
large language models trained?

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.000
Sort of building on that previous
answer, the big difference

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.430
between a large language model
that you might have heard

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.250
of like GPT-3 and the language
model that sort of sits there

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.830
doing predictive text on your
phone is really just the scale

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.200
of the data that it's been
trained on and the biggest models

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.450
that we have right now
have really been trained

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.420
more or less on most of
the text on the internet.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.912
They're sort of enormous machine
learning models with really

00:01:30.912 --> 00:01:32.941
really high capacity that
have just been sort of trained

00:01:32.941 --> 00:01:35.341
to do that same next word
prediction task but on a much,

00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:36.900
much, much broader diversity
of documents set of

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.064
kinds of text languages
of documents and so on

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:41.914
than the one that sits on your phone.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.060
What are some of the key
technical innovations

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.150
that have enabled the development
of large language models?

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.490
So language models, it's
important to note have been around

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.246
for a long time, really since
the very earliest days of

00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:55.890
natural language processing.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.410
So when people were first
setting out to build

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.200
automatic speech recognition
systems, really sort of

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.240
at the dawn of computer
science there was always

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.130
this problem of figuring
out, well if I have

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.560
a couple of different ways
of interpreting the audio

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.140
that I'm hearing, which of
those is actually likely

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.240
to be the sequence of
words that people meant?

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.820
And even then, people sort
of trained these kinds

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.710
of predictive text models
that could look at a sentence

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.780
and guess what word was
coming or tell you whether

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.130
a sentence was likely or not.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.350
And so these have been with
us for a very long time

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.090
and the reason that I think
most people are hearing

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.970
about them now, or the big
thing that's sort of different

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.420
in the last three or five
years is just sort of

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.520
how much bigger and how
much better these models

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.098
have gotten.

00:02:34.098 --> 00:02:38.730
of widespread use of
artificial neural network

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.940
machine learning models as
models for language processing.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.280
These have also been around,
really for a long time,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.980
like since the sixties
but various developments

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.840
have kind of converged in
the last couple of years,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.430
That means that we're able to train them

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.600
at a very large scale,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.610
we're able to train them
on a large amount of data

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.800
and they just work super,
super well and so what we're

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.960
seeing with sort of large
language models today

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.630
is a sort of convergence
of better hardware

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.180
for training neural network
models, some basic innovation

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.100
in how those neural
network models are trained

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.770
and then just enormous amounts
of data on the internet

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.403
that allows us to train them
at a really large scale,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.540
how do language models differ
from traditional approaches

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.460
to natural language processing?

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.710
So maybe one of the
other biggest differences

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.600
between these models that
we're seeing right now

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.580
and even the kinds of
language models that people

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.680
were building, you know when
I was in graduate school

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.550
not very long ago is that
we bake very little about

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.400
the structure of natural
language into them ahead of time

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.070
and so it used to be that
a large part of the work

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.280
in natural language processing
was sort of taking our

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.910
understanding of how languages
are structured, how sort of

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.240
syntax works, how the morphology
of individual words work

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.580
and building that into specialized
machine learning models

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.700
that can used this information
to make better predictions.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.430
And with many of the kinds
of models that we're training

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.530
right now you don't
necessarily have to bake all

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.930
of that information in from
the beginning and instead

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.160
these models learn just from reading large

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.240
or I guess training on
large amounts of data

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.430
what the structure of
language is and how it works.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.200
Now this is not to say
that sort of knowledge

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.510
about linguistic structure
is not useful for NLP,

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.780
it's certainly the case that
in languages without large

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.810
digitized training sets or
with small numbers of speakers

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.120
being able to build models
that really do have access

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.910
to sort of rich information
about linguistic structure

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.080
is still important.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.330
And our understanding
of how language works

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.280
can inform the ways in
which we test these models

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.380
to make sure that they're
working and the ways we sort of

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.540
probe their internal
reprensantations to figure

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.740
out how it is they work.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.630
But I guess the biggest
difference between the way

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.730
we're doing things now and
the way we were doing things

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.190
even five years ago or 10
years ago, is that most

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.490
of that structure sort of
gets used after the fact

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.850
and not when we are training
models from the onset.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.200
What are some of the challenges involved

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.720
in training and deploying
large language models?

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.480
And maybe the biggest challenge
which I've been alluding to

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.930
a little bit already is just
the fact that these models

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.710
require huge amounts of
computing power to train

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.180
and so nowadays the kinds of
models that you've heard about

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.440
things like, like ChatGPT or
Llama have been trained on

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.020
hundreds or thousands of
GPUs for hours and hours

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.560
and hours and hours and
this presents a couple of

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:14.730
different challenges.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.250
It both just requires an
enormous amount of energy

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.740
to train these models, the
training process itself

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.410
is complicated, it's sort
of the idea of training

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:26.537
a machine learning model at
the scale for such a long time

00:05:26.537 --> 00:05:30.390
is not something that we've
done until relatively recently

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.190
in the machine learning world or in the

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.680
natural language processing
world and so a lot of the

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.780
challenge really comes in training.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.652
And I think this is
part of the reason that,

00:05:39.652 --> 00:05:42.578
there are only a small
number of these models

00:05:42.578 --> 00:05:44.640
out there that you know
sort of most people are

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.590
interacting with or that
most people have heard of

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.450
because training even
one of them is just like

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.270
a really complicated and
really costly endeavor

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:54.900
requiring huge teams of people
and hundreds of thousands

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.360
or millions of dollars
and as there's a result is

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.970
the sort of thing that
people are only doing

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.203
relatively infrequently.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.890
Why do the most prominent
large language models

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.050
all have effectively the same architecture

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.020
and what is the architecture
of a transformer model?

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.660
Many of the biggest language
models that we're sort

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.450
of seeing released to the
public right now are based

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.640
on a particular neural
network architecture called

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.220
the Transformer that was
introduced around 2017.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.180
And the way a transformer
works is basically

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.940
you sort of take a chunk of text as input

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.460
whether it's a sentence or
a paragraph or a document

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.190
and you look at individual
words or sometimes pieces

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.230
of words in that document
and you represent them

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:38.580
as actually points in a very,
very high dimensional space.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.760
You then apply a series of
operations that are called

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.070
attention to those points
where every point sort of looks

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.040
at all of its other neighbors
decides to wiggle itself

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.170
around a little bit in
that space and then all

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.580
of the neighbors do the same thing.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.040
And you repeat this process
over and over and over again

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.320
till eventually you sort
of take one of those points

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.360
you figure out which
word it's now closest to

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.179
which is probably not the
word that it started out

00:07:00.179 --> 00:07:01.710
closest to and that
becomes the model's output

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:02.850
at the next time step.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.070
These transformer models
are not the only way to

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.782
build a language model or
even a neural network based

00:07:08.782 --> 00:07:10.560
language model and one of
the reasons that I think

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.470
we're seeing such a dominance
of this kind of transformer

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.360
architecture in the last couple of years

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.120
is that they're really easy
to train at a large scale.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.360
And so I mentioned before that
these sorts of computational

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.140
constraints are one of the big
issues or the big challenges

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.777
that come up when training
these language models

00:07:27.777 --> 00:07:29.460
and these transformer type models turn

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.400
out to be particularly
well suited to training

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.680
in a distributed way,
training at a large scale

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.510
and I think that's one of the
reasons that we've started

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.310
to see so much of them right now.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.920
But it's important to mention
that's not the only way

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.020
to build a language model
and it wouldn't surprise me

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.840
if in the next couple of
years we actually start to see

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.770
greater diversity in the kinds
of neural language models

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.270
even that people are training.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.990
What are some promising
ways to use these models

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.210
and especially ways of
using these models beyond

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.850
the language and code
applications that many people

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.080
have seen?

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.810
Taking a step back, why are
we here, why are we doing this

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:04.643
explainer?

00:08:04.643 --> 00:08:06.630
Why is there so much
interest in language models

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:07.463
in the first place?

00:08:07.463 --> 00:08:10.680
And I think a big part of this
is that, starting with this

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.710
very simple task of
predicting what word is going

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:17.070
to come next in a document
turns out to give models

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.770
that are capable of
accomplishing a wide variety

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.110
of other useful things right?

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.930
So sort of mentioning ways of language app

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.550
or using these models in the context

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:29.880
of language applications, if
I am trying to write an essay

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.730
and I type in a question
or a prompt that says

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:36.240
something like what were the
causes of the first world war?

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.700
A good guess at what kind of
language is gonna come next

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.860
if you're a good auto
complete model is an essay

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.830
or is a sort of detailed
answer that actually addresses

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:44.663
those questions.

00:08:44.663 --> 00:08:46.620
And what this means is
that a lot of things that

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.350
we haven't sort of
traditionally thought of as

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.570
things that language models
are for or even things

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.250
that are kind of within the domain of

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.470
natural language processing
as a field to tackle

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.720
you can actually pose as
these kinds of next word

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.970
prediction problems with
appropriate prompts.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.860
And what this means is that we
can use these language models

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.966
to do a ton of different things.

00:09:03.966 --> 00:09:07.080
And so as the question gets
at a lot of the applications

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.590
that people have seen so
far really build directly

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.050
on this kind of text
auto complete paradigm,

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:16.050
you're sitting in your text
editor in your Microsoft Word

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.270
or Google Docs, you type
in some instructions

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.250
or you type in the sort
of beginning of a document

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.470
and you ask the model to
guess what's gonna come next.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.920
And sometimes that's the text
or at least close to the text

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.590
that you already wanted
to write and similarly

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.900
in these kinds of programming
applications right,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.960
you type in a comment and if
you have a really good model

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.731
that model can often guess
what code should come

00:09:36.731 --> 00:09:38.256
at come after that comment.

00:09:38.256 --> 00:09:40.140
Especially if it's really,
the sort of boiler plate code

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.810
that many of us find ourselves
writing over and over again

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.180
that we know other people out
there have already written

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.970
many times and maybe even
posted inversions that exist

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:48.990
on the internet.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:53.160
All that said I think we're
likely to see these models

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.680
showing up in all kinds of
other interesting places,

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.350
even in sort of problems that
on the surface maybe seem

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:01.080
not to have anything to do
with text or anything to do

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.820
with programming at all.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.722
So one thing that my group,
for example has been thinking

00:10:05.722 --> 00:10:08.430
a lot about recently is
what we can learn from text

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.220
or what we can sort of
extract from language models

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.350
about how the world works in a general way

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.690
that we can then use to
support computer vision

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:18.450
or sort of improve models
for robotics or models

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:19.440
for computer vision.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.150
So if you're trying to
accomplish some sort of

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.440
difficult household task, cleaning a room,

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.380
cooking a complicated meal,
there's a lot of common sense

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.300
knowledge that's sort
of difficult to get from

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.820
traditional robotics or
computer vision training sets,

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.670
what kinds of objects are
likely to co-occur together?

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.250
What sensible place in
your living room is to set

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:39.960
down this pillow that you just picked up,

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.060
where in the kitchen you're
likely to find a teapot

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.830
or tea bags or things like that.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.170
And what's amazing is that
language models, again just

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.630
by sort of virtue of doing
this next word prediction task

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.480
know a lot of these things as
well and so if you query them

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.430
in the right ways and
at the right times, you

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.130
can actually use them to
fix or improve or guide

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:59.130
the outputs, even if models
for like robotics tasks

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:00.870
and computer vision tasks
where there might not

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.990
actually be any language
as an input or as an output

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.430
to the model.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.370
How do multilingual transformers
handle different languages

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.020
and dialects?

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.750
You know, one other thing
that we're starting to see

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.120
in the models that people
are training right now

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.130
or at least in many of
the largest scale models

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.640
is that they're trained not
just on a single language

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.620
but on many, many different languages,

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.320
maybe as many different
languages as we can get our

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:26.153
hands on.

00:11:26.153 --> 00:11:28.380
And this is a really
important development for

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.770
a long time in the history of
natural language processing

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.312
as a field, a lot of the
research was being done

00:11:34.312 --> 00:11:37.380
just in English sort of building
models that were trained

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.110
only on data in English or even
that worked only on English

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.090
even if you tried to train
them on other data sets.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:45.390
Now this is certainly, not
what everybody's been doing

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.550
and there's a long sort
of history going back

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.040
to the earliest days of the
field of trying to do NLP

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.000
for other languages.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.890
But if you just look at
most of the papers that are

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:56.935
getting published at our
sort of top NLP conferences

00:11:56.935 --> 00:12:00.540
a lot of the work is in English
and so one thing that we see

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:04.590
when we train these models
on multilingual data is that,

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:07.260
not only do they sort of
acquire the ability to do all

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:08.940
of these impressive
things, not just in English

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.160
but in other languages
as well but that actually

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.370
you often benefit especially
in the smallest languages

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.130
by sort of training models
at this large scale.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.340
So there's a sense in which
models can sort of take skills

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.950
that they acquired from tasks
that were specified in English

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.130
and the training data and can
generalize those to languages

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:29.220
that maybe were sort of so
sparsely represented in the data

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.950
that they don't have examples
of every individual task

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:33.090
that a model is solving.

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:36.990
And so I guess an
important feature of these

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.260
multilingual transformers
is just that they not only

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:41.970
can they handle different
languages and dialects,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.300
but that they get better
especially at those sort of

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.450
least represented languages
and dialects as these scales

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:49.680
get bigger.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.990
Now, an important caveat
there of course is that

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.230
our performance with these
models in English is still

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.780
far ahead of most of the
other languages that we can

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:01.590
train them on, there's still
enormous need for technologies

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:03.630
that serve people that
are not sort of speakers

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:06.060
of the world's five
most frequent languages

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.130
or users of those languages.

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:11.760
And while these multilingual
models are an important

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.950
first step in that direction,
I think there's still a lot

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.170
of work to do both on the modeling side

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.420
and really especially on
the benchmarking side,

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:20.790
just to give us a kind of
clear picture of how well

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.340
these tools work outside
of English and especially

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:27.120
in less common languages,
what are the limitations

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:30.120
of large language models and
how can they be addressed?

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.700
This is a big question and you
know I think one of the most

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.400
important ones is what we
were talking about just

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:38.130
a minute ago with regard to
sort of how well they work

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.440
in different languages and even
just understanding how well

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.420
they work for different kinds of users.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:46.410
But there are also sort
of user specific questions

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.110
aside a couple of like
really fundamental issues

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:51.210
that even the biggest
models that we have today

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:54.330
still face one of them
has to do with factuality,

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:58.050
so it's well documented that
these models hallucinate facts

00:13:58.050 --> 00:13:59.700
that they're not always reliable.

00:14:00.559 --> 00:14:03.180
I can't remember why I was
doing this, but recently

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.960
asked ChatGPT to describe
some of our group's research

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.710
and it named five papers
four of which are not papers

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.750
that actually exist and one
of which is a real paper

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.480
that was written by a
colleague of mine who lives

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.734
in the United Kingdom who
I've never co-authored with,

00:14:18.734 --> 00:14:20.940
so factuality is still a big problem.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:24.802
And even sort of beyond that
things involving reasoning

00:14:24.802 --> 00:14:26.940
in a really general sense things involving

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.060
sort of complicated computations,
complicated inferences

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.310
still seem to be really, really
difficult for these models

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:35.310
and might be even sort of
fundamental limitations

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.590
of this transformer
architecture and are I think

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:40.552
somewhere where a lot more
modeling work is needed

00:14:40.552 --> 00:14:43.890
to make things better,
can large language models

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.630
understand context?

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.270
Also an interesting question
and I think depends on

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:51.330
a lot on what you mean
by context, so certainly

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:53.700
when we think about linguistic
contexts these models

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:56.520
are capable of sort of reasoning
about much, much longer

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:00.000
documents and sort of
chunks of text more broadly

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.850
than really anything that we've
known how to build before.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:07.230
But that's only one kind
of context and people write

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.020
production language comprehension
takes place in sort of

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.160
grounded context.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.590
That I know I'm sitting at
this table there are objects

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:15.510
that I can refer to
and the language models

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:18.360
that we have right now,
when they're interacting

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.700
with a human user typically
can't see any of that.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.340
There's a broader social
context that informs a lot

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.520
of our language use and also
that these models are at least

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:29.280
not sort of immediately sensitive
to or where it's not clear

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.190
how to give them information
about the social context

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.955
in which their language
generation and language modeling

00:15:34.955 --> 00:15:35.788
takes place.

00:15:35.788 --> 00:15:37.620
Another important thing is,
I guess temporal context,

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.990
that we're sort of shooting this video

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:42.360
at a particular moment in
time when particular facts

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:43.470
are true.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.810
The models that we have right
now, we're sort of trained

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.256
on, again a snapshot of
the internet that stopped

00:15:49.256 --> 00:15:51.030
at a particular time.

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:52.980
For most models that we
have now probably a couple

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.020
of years ago and they
don't know about anything

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:56.790
that's happened since then,
they don't even know sort of

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.460
at what moment in time
they're doing text generation

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.100
and so figuring out how
to provide all of those

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.853
different kinds of contexts is
also an interesting question.

